 / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Mehrangarh is a fort above the city of Jodhpur in Rajasthan, India, founded in 1459 by Rao Jodha of the Rathore dynasty as the new capital of Marwar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mehri-language"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mehri-language.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mehri language</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Mehri is a Modern South Arabian language spoken by the Mahra people of eastern Yemen and western Oman, and by migrants in the Gulf states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mehsana-district"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mehsana-district.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mehsana district</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mehsana district is a district of northern Gujarat, India, with its headquarters at Mehsana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/meic-uilleim"><span class="enc-name">Meic Uilleim</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Meic Uilleim, or MacWilliams, were descendants of William fitz Duncan, a grandson of Malcolm III, who claimed the Scottish throne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meidum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meidum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meidum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meidum is an archaeological site in Egypt on the desert edge west of the Nile, about 100 km south of Cairo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meifod"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meifod.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meifod</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meifod is a village in the Vyrnwy valley of Montgomeryshire, mid Wales.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meigle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meigle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meigle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meigle is a village in Strathmore, Perthshire, Scotland, with one of the most important collections of early medieval carved stones in Europe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meiji-constitution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meiji Constitution</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Meiji Constitution was the constitution of the Empire of Japan, promulgated in 1889.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meiji-restoration.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meiji Restoration</span><span class="enc-type">Revolution · 1868</span><span class="enc-snip">The Meiji Restoration was the political revolution of 1868 that ended the Tokugawa Shogunate and restored direct imperial rule under Emperor Meiji.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/meilyr-brydydd"><span class="enc-name">Meilyr Brydydd</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Meilyr Brydydd was a Welsh court poet, the earliest of the Poets of the Princes whose work survives.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meinhard-ii-of-gorizia-tyrol"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meinhard-ii-of-gorizia-tyrol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meinhard II of Gorizia-Tyrol</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Meinhard II was count of Gorizia and Tyrol who, in the family division of 1271, took Tyrol and left Gorizia to his brother Albert.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meiningen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meiningen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meiningen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meiningen is a town in southern Thuringia, Germany, on the Werra between the Thuringian Forest and the Rhön.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/meiningen-ensemble"><span class="enc-name">Meiningen Ensemble</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Meiningen Ensemble was the theatre company of the court of Saxe-Meiningen, directed by Duke George II with Ludwig Chronegk and Ellen Franz.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meinwerk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meinwerk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meinwerk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Meinwerk was bishop of Paderborn from 1009 to 1036, a Saxon noble and kinsman of Emperor Henry II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meisenheim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meisenheim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meisenheim</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meisenheim is a small town on the Glan in Rhineland-Palatinate, with a well-preserved medieval centre.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meissen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meissen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meissen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meissen is a town on the Elbe in Saxony, Germany, north-west of Dresden.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meknes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meknes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meknes</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meknes is a city in northern Morocco, founded as a Berber settlement in the 10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mekong-delta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mekong Delta</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mekong Delta is the region of southern Vietnam where the Mekong River divides into many channels before reaching the South China Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mekong-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mekong River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mekong is a major river of South-East Asia, flowing from Tibet through China, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/melayu-islam-beraja"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/melayu-islam-beraja.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Melayu Islam Beraja</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Melayu Islam Beraja, or Malay Islamic Monarchy, is the national ideology of Brunei, proclaimed at independence in 1984.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/melbourne"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/melbourne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Melbourne</span><span class="enc-type">Place