bey</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Mellifont Abbey, in County Louth, was the first Cistercian monastery in Ireland, founded in 1142 by Saint Malachy with monks from Clairvaux, under the patronage of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mellitus"><span class="enc-name">Mellitus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mellitus was a member of the Gregorian mission sent to England in 601, who became the first bishop of London in 604 under King Sæberht of Essex.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/melmoth-osborn"><span class="enc-name">Melmoth Osborn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir Melmoth Osborn was a British colonial official in Natal and Zululand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/melnik"><span class="enc-name">Melnik</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Melnik is a small town in south-western Bulgaria, near the Struma valley, set among sandstone pillars known as the Melnik pyramids.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/melqart"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/melqart.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Melqart</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Melqart was the chief god of the Phoenician city of Tyre, whose name means 'king of the city'.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/melrose-abbey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/melrose-abbey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Melrose Abbey</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Melrose Abbey is a ruined Cistercian abbey in the Scottish Borders, founded by David I in 1136, the first Cistercian house in Scotland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meluhha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meluhha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meluhha</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meluhha was a land known to ancient Mesopotamians, generally identified with the Indus Valley civilisation.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/melus-of-bari"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/melus-of-bari.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Melus of Bari</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Melus of Bari was a Lombard nobleman of Apulia who led revolts against Byzantine rule in southern Italy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/melville-herskovits"><span class="enc-name">Melville Herskovits</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Melville Herskovits was an American anthropologist who founded the first major African studies programme in the United States at Northwestern University in 1948.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/melvin-evans"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/melvin-evans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Melvin Evans</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Melvin Evans was a physician and politician from the United States Virgin Islands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mem-and-zin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mem-and-zin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mem and Zin</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Mem and Zin is an epic poem in Kurmanji Kurdish written by Ahmad Khani and completed in 1692.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mem-de-sa"><span class="enc-name">Mem de Sá</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mem de Sá was the third governor-general of Portuguese Brazil, serving from 1557 until his death in 1572.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mema"><span class="enc-name">Mema</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Mema was a Soninke kingdom on the middle Niger in West Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/memnon-of-heraclea"><span class="enc-name">Memnon of Heraclea</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Memnon of Heraclea was a Greek historian, probably of the 1st or 2nd century CE, who wrote a history of his city, Heraclea Pontica.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/memory-of-the-world-programme.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Memory of the World Programme</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Memory of the World Programme is an initiative launched by UNESCO in 1992 to protect the world's documentary heritage and make it accessible.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mempawah"><span class="enc-name">Mempawah</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mempawah is a coastal town in West Kalimantan, Indonesia, north of Pontianak, and the seat of a regency of the same name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/memphis-egypt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Memphis, Egypt</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Memphis was the capital of ancient Egypt for much of its history, founded around 3100 BCE where the Nile valley meets the Delta, south of modern Cairo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/men-